# Copy to .env.local (gitignored) and fill in. Names only here — never values.
# Root .env.local is the single source of truth; npm scripts inject it via dotenv-cli.
# Mirror these into Vercel project settings before the first deploy.

# --- Database (Neon Postgres) — B-001/B-002 ---
# Pooled connection used by the app at runtime.
# The Neon endpoint id the DESTRUCTIVE local scripts expect to be pointed at —
# `db:migrate:test`, `db:seed:demo` and `db:create-owner`. Set it to the same
# endpoint as DATABASE_URL below, once.
#
# It exists because pasting a production connection string into this file is
# the obvious mistake to make the day a deployment exists, and the very next
# `npm run db:migrate:test` would create a test schema inside the production
# database and run the suite against it. Changing the connection strings leaves
# this line alone, which is exactly the mismatch that stops it.
#
# Leave it unset and those scripts behave as they always have.
EXPECTED_DEV_DB_HOST=

DATABASE_URL=
# Direct (non-pooled) connection used by Prisma Migrate.
DIRECT_URL=

# --- Auth.js — B-003 ---
# Signing key for session JWTs. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_SECRET=
# Origin used to build magic-link and password-reset URLs.
AUTH_URL=

# --- Background jobs — B-006 ---
# Shared secret for the /api/cron endpoint. Vercel Cron sends it as a Bearer
# token; the route rejects every request when this is unset.
CRON_SECRET=

# --- Added in later backlog items; listed so nothing is a surprise ---
# Stripe, test mode only (B-019)
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- Communications — B-030 ---
# Resend API key for transactional email. Unset (dev/test/preview) falls back to
# a log-only provider that records the full Message evidence row but sends no
# real mail — the same honest-degradation posture as STRIPE_SECRET_KEY. A real
# key is used only in production, or in a non-prod env that also sets
# COMMS_SANDBOX_INBOX (below); otherwise a stray key cannot reach a tenant.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# PRD 05 FR-14. Signing secret for Resend's delivery webhook (Svix), posted to
# /api/comms/webhook. Without it that route fails closed with 503 — a forged
# `email.bounced` would suppress a real tenant's address and cut them off from
# every notice this system sends.
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Sending domain for the From address (needs SPF/DKIM/DMARC set up in DNS).
# COMMS_EMAIL_DOMAIN=mail.example.com
# Org-level kill switch (FR-20). Set to "on" to pause ALL outbound immediately.
# Emergency stop, not a scheduled pause: messages during the pause are not sent
# and are not replayed when it clears.
# COMMS_KILL_SWITCH=
# Non-production safety net (FR-20): with a real RESEND_API_KEY outside
# production, every recipient is rewritten to this inbox so no real tenant can
# be emailed from a preview/staging deploy.
# COMMS_SANDBOX_INBOX=

# --- SMS — B-074 ---
# Twilio account credentials. Unset (dev/test/preview) falls back to a
# log-only SMS provider, same honest-degradation posture as RESEND_API_KEY —
# every reminder still records its Message evidence row, no real text goes
# out. A real credential is used only in production, or in a non-prod env
# that also sets COMMS_SANDBOX_SMS; otherwise a stray credential cannot text
# a real tenant. Each facility's own Messaging Service SID is a database
# setting (Facility.smsMessagingServiceSid, admin settings screen), not an
# env var — one Twilio account, one number pool per facility.
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# Non-production safety net, SMS side: with real Twilio credentials outside
# production, every recipient phone is rewritten to this number so no real
# tenant can be texted from a preview/staging deploy.
# COMMS_SANDBOX_SMS=

# --- Stripe — B-019 ---
# Secret API key. Test keys (sk_test_...) everywhere except production.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# Publishable key for the Payment Element in the browser.
NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
# Signing secret for /api/stripe/webhook, from the Stripe dashboard or
# `stripe listen`. The endpoint refuses every request when this is unset —
# without it we cannot tell a real delivery from anyone else's POST.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- Marketplace lead attribution — B-082 part 1 ---
# Partner name → shared key, as a JSON object:
#   MARKETPLACE_LEAD_KEYS={"sparefoot":"<key>","storable":"<key>"}
#
# Which key authenticates decides which partner an inbound lead is credited to
# (POST /api/public/marketplace/leads). The request body cannot name its own
# channel — attribution is what an aggregator invoices against, so a body that
# could claim a channel could claim a competitor's.
#
# Unset means no partner authenticates and the endpoint answers 401 to
# everything, which is the correct closed default. A malformed value does the
# same rather than degrading to an empty key that every caller would match.
# The availability feed beside it (/api/public/marketplace/availability) needs
# nothing here — it publishes only what is already on the public site.
MARKETPLACE_LEAD_KEYS=

# --- Google Maps — B-107, D-46 ---
# Map RENDERING only. Geocoding stays on the bundled dataset (D-14), so search
# still resolves offline and none of this is on the search hot path.
#
# Both values ship to the browser and are readable in the page source. That is
# unavoidable for a Maps JavaScript key, which is why the key MUST be
# HTTP-referrer restricted to our own hosts and scoped to the Maps JavaScript
# API alone — an unrestricted one is a billable resource anybody can copy out.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=
# Map ID from the Google Cloud console. Required by AdvancedMarkerElement, which
# is what lets a price bubble be real HTML — and therefore a real link, with a
# real contrast ratio and a real place in the tab order. Not a secret.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_MAP_ID=
# With either unset the search page renders no map at all and the results list
# is unchanged. That is the intended degraded state, not an error.

# --- Gate hardware simulator — B-028 ---
# Signing secret for /api/hardware/webhook. Optional outside production: an
# unset value there gets a random per-process secret automatically, because
# nothing external calls this route yet (only the in-process simulator) — see
# hardwareWebhookSecret()'s own note. Set a real value once a real vendor's
# webhook needs to reach this endpoint (B-085).
# HARDWARE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- Gate code encryption — B-029 ---
# AES-256-GCM key (32 bytes, hex) for at-rest gate-code storage, so a code can
# later be shown on the confirmation page or revealed to staff. Generate with:
# openssl rand -hex 32
# Unlike HARDWARE_WEBHOOK_SECRET there is no per-process dev fallback — this
# key has to outlive the process that encrypted a code to ever decrypt it
# again. Unset means "no code can be shown," the same honest-degradation
# posture as an unconfigured STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, falling back to "texted
# within 15 minutes."
# ACCESS_CODE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Vercel Blob, for files a tenant uploads (proof of insurance).
# Created with `vercel blob store add` and exposed automatically on Vercel;
# set it locally only if you want uploads to work in development. Without it
# the details a tenant types are still recorded and the file is refused with a
# message saying so, rather than silently dropped.
BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=
